

⭐ CMB VIEWER SETTINGS SYSTEM (Final Form)

The settings system has one purpose:

Store viewer preferences → apply them instantly → persist them cleanly

It does NOT:

affect simulation data

modify blueprints

change physics

It only controls how the viewer behaves and renders.

The entire system is built around three layers:

Settings Schema

Settings Store

Settings UI

Nothing more.

⭐ 1. Settings Schema (The Contract)

This is the single source of truth for all viewer settings.

It’s a simple JSON object:

{
  "playback": {...},
  "overlays": {...},
  "viewport": {...},
  "hud": {...},
  "performance": {...}
}

Each category contains flat, predictable keys.

Example:

playback: {
  "speed": 1.0,
  "loop": false
}

overlays: {
  "theta": true,
  "xi": true,
  "organisms": true,
  "metric": false
}

viewport: {
  "zoom": 1.0,
  "pan": [0, 0]
}

hud: {
  "mode": "scientific"
}

performance: {
  "resolutionScale": 1.0,
  "gpuAcceleration": true
}

This schema defines everything the viewer can control.

⭐ 2. Settings Store (The Engine)

The Settings Store is a tiny state manager.

It does three things:

A. Load settings

From:

local storage

a JSON file

defaults

B. Apply settings

Immediately update:

overlays

viewport

HUD

playback

performance

C. Save settings

Persist changes automatically.

🔷 Settings Store API (Final Form)

Settings.get(key)
Settings.set(key, value)
Settings.reset()
Settings.load()
Settings.save()

That’s the entire API.

No events.No reducers.No frameworks.

Just get / set / load / save.

⭐ 3. Settings UI (The Control Surface)

The UI is a single panel with collapsible sections:

Playback

Overlays

Viewport

HUD

Performance

Each setting maps directly to a key in the schema.

No hidden logic.No derived values.No magic.

⭐ 4. Category Breakdown (Minimal, Complete)

🔷 Playback Settings

speed

loop

frame stepping behavior

These affect how time moves.

🔷 Overlay Settings

Each overlay has:

enabled

opacity

detail level

color map

These affect what you see.

🔷 Viewport Settings

zoom

pan

fit mode (fit, fill, 1:1)

These affect how you see it.

🔷 HUD Settings

mode (minimal, scientific, blueprint)

font size

position

These affect information density.

🔷 Performance Settings

resolution scale

GPU acceleration

overlay quality

These affect rendering speed.

⭐ 5. Persistence Model

Settings are saved to:

localStorage["cmb_viewer_settings"]

or a simple JSON file:

settings.json

This makes the viewer:

stateless

portable

predictable

No databases.No cloud sync.No accounts.

⭐ 6. Reset Behavior

A single button:

Reset Viewer Settings

Restores:

default playback

default overlays

default HUD

default viewport

default performance

This is essential for debugging and clean sessions.



